WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.533
Across the globe,

00:00:01.533 --> 00:00:03.820
companies and organizations
began shifting production

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.790
to meet the demand for
much-needed ventilators.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.720
In Europe, scuba diving masks

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.800
became a new tool to fight COVID-19.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.540
An Israeli missile company

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.100
now makes life-saving breathing machines.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.530
And, in the United States,

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.690
Tesla engineers designed ventilators

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.320
made from spare electric car parts.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.200
In late March, Elon
Musk, the CEO of Tesla,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.150
offered free ventilators to
hospitals that needed them.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.360
But, these machines,
designed for sleep apnea,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.040
had to be converted by doctors

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.640
at a New York City hospital.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.960
Now, Tesla employees created a prototype

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.020
made from Model 3 parts.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.800
- We've been working on developing

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.080
our own ventilator
design, specifically one

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.340
that's heavily based on Tesla car parts.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.840
You can see that all the red parts

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.130
here are a Tesla vehicle part,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.377
so we've used a lot of those.

00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:53.680
- This touch screen is powered by

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.990
the Model 3 infotainment computer in here,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.620
which controls Model
3 vehicle controllers.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.320
- In Michigan, Ford and GE

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.200
plan to use a former automotive plant

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.910
to build a ventilator that
doesn't need electricity.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.660
It uses air pressure to run.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.010
The companies expect to
get started in late April,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.390
producing 50,000 ventilators
in the first 100 days,

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.350
and then, 30,000 a month, going forward.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.120
In Tokyo, Metron produces
ventilators for animals.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.830
Now, they're being
converted to use for people.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.840
The CEO said the respiratory systems

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.240
of humans and many animals are similar.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.750
Japan's government asked Metron

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.140
to modify the equipment for human use.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.570
Representatives from the
UK, the US, and India

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.500
have also reached out to
the company to ask for help.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.420
The global effort to fight the coronavirus

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.550
even meant one company
making a life-saving product,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.350
instead of its usual life-taking weapons.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.900
In Tel Aviv, Israel Aerospace Industries

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.580
rapidly shifted its production line

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.120
from missiles to ventilators.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.630
It partnered with medical
device maker Inovytec

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.100
and the Israel Defense Force

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.540
to deliver 30 ventilators.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.930
In France, the Ambroise Paré Clinic

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.410
converted scuba diving
masks to medical use,

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.290
so that patients would not need tubes

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.830
going into their lungs.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.540
These can help less
severe cases of COVID-19.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.450
Decathlon, the maker of the masks,

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:12.930
suspended sales to the public

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.883
and donated them to hospitals in need.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.220
In Belgium, an engineer devised an adapter

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.740
for the masks on a 3D printing machine.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.090
- So that's why we
could go very, very fast

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.350
and from the first talk we had

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.820
to the really usable prototype,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.380
it was really a few hours.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.370
- The printed prototype

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.030
became the model for a large order

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:34.883
using medical-grade plastics.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.070
In the Czech Republic,

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.000
researchers designed a simple ventilator

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.940
for others to produce around the world.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.500
The design will be released to the public

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.840
so that anyone can
manufacture the ventilators,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.450
instead of waiting for
the finished product

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.210
to be shipped from somewhere else.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.350
And in Slovakia,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.370
these students built a stop-gap ventilator

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.740
for patients waiting on
more professional designs.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.910
In light of the pandemic,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.530
the world is seeing human
ingenuity and compassion

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.330
attacking a problem facing everyone.

